WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.784
If a cheap model writes the code, and an expensive model checks

00:00:03.784 --> 00:00:05.152
it, who checks the checker?

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.149
Hi, I'm Ulric!

00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:13.583
Today it's improve, a skill by Shad C N that splits coding

00:00:13.583 --> 00:00:15.873
between a strong model and a cheap one.

00:00:15.873 --> 00:00:17.591
Let's read what it actually does.

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:19.980
Chapter one.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.576
The plan is the product.

00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:29.458
The advisor is whatever strong model your session runs.

00:00:29.458 --> 00:00:33.478
It reads the repo, and it is never allowed to edit source code.

00:00:33.898 --> 00:00:37.508
It audits, vets every finding against the code itself, and writes one

00:00:37.508 --> 00:00:40.216
plan per finding you pick, into a plans folder.

00:00:40.636 --> 00:00:44.762
With execute, a cheaper subagent gets the whole plan pasted into its

00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:47.168
prompt and edits a disposable git worktree.

00:00:47.588 --> 00:00:49.526
Then the advisor reviews the result.

00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:50.172
And merging?

00:00:50.172 --> 00:00:51.787
That always stays with you.

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:55.132
Here is everything in it.

00:00:55.132 --> 00:00:58.827
The skill file sets the role: a senior advisor, not an implementer.

00:00:59.247 --> 00:01:02.760
Three references: how to audit, how to write a plan for the

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.395
weakest plausible executor, and how to close the loop.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:09.567
One real example plan, and a Claude Code plugin manifest.

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.443
There is no program anywhere.

00:01:11.443 --> 00:01:12.944
The product is instructions.

00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:15.250
Chapter two.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.688
What the check really is.

00:01:21.142 --> 00:01:22.012
Here's the idea.

00:01:22.012 --> 00:01:23.753
The cheap model types the plan.

00:01:23.753 --> 00:01:26.945
Then the same strong model that wrote it re-runs its checks

00:01:26.945 --> 00:01:28.106
and grades the diff.

00:01:30.158 --> 00:01:34.078
The plan inlines everything: why it matters, excerpts of the current code,

00:01:34.078 --> 00:01:37.344
steps that each end in a command, and stop conditions.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:41.965
The executor checks for drift first, then edits, runs the tests, and reports.

00:01:41.965 --> 00:01:45.197
Here it also touched a file the plan never listed.

00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:50.095
The advisor re-runs every done criterion itself, then diffs the worktree against

00:01:50.095 --> 00:01:51.587
the plan's file list.

00:01:51.587 --> 00:01:53.826
Out of scope fails, full stop.

00:01:53.826 --> 00:01:54.199
Revise.

00:01:54.619 --> 00:01:55.300
Round two.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.386
The executor fixes it, every gate passes, and the verdict is approve.

00:01:59.386 --> 00:02:02.109
The skill allows at most two revision rounds.

00:02:02.529 --> 00:02:03.965
Even approved, nothing is merged.

00:02:03.965 --> 00:02:05.975
You get a branch and a summary.

00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:12.614
The repo's example plan runs to one thousand one hundred and forty-nine words.

00:02:12.614 --> 00:02:15.965
A plan is a document the strong model pays for.

00:02:16.385 --> 00:02:19.799
Execution is the long loop of edits, test runs and re-reads.

00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:22.903
This is the only part moved to the cheap model.

00:02:23.323 --> 00:02:26.557
The review reads the plan, the diff and the test logs again,

00:02:26.557 --> 00:02:27.904
at the strong model's price.

00:02:28.324 --> 00:02:31.721
Every revision round repeats some execution and a whole review.

00:02:33.342 --> 00:02:33.951
Chapter three.

00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:35.170
Does it save money?

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:44.034
On this site's list prices, Opus five point five costs only twice

00:02:44.034 --> 00:02:46.884
what Sonnet five point five does per token.

00:02:46.884 --> 00:02:49.022
And Sonnet is the default executor.

00:02:50.425 --> 00:02:54.732
On a refactor I sized myself, the Sonnet split saves twenty-seven percent

00:02:54.732 --> 00:02:56.167
on a clean pass.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:59.756
One revision round, and it costs more than Opus alone.

00:02:59.756 --> 00:03:01.550
With Haiku, the saving survives.

00:03:03.192 --> 00:03:04.953
Some faults get past every gate.

00:03:05.781 --> 00:03:08.761
The drift check only diffs the files a plan names.

00:03:08.761 --> 00:03:11.443
Change a caller it never listed, and nothing notices.

00:03:11.863 --> 00:03:15.605
The plan, its done criteria and the review all come from one model.

00:03:15.605 --> 00:03:18.772
If it misread the code, all three agree with the mistake.

00:03:19.192 --> 00:03:21.912
And only a careful read catches a test written to pass.

00:03:23.142 --> 00:03:26.973
The split pays when execution dwarfs the plan and the review.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:31.848
So pick plans with long, mechanical execution, and an executor that is really cheap.

00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:33.885
To recap.

00:03:33.885 --> 00:03:35.962
The advisor plans and never edits.

00:03:35.962 --> 00:03:38.040
The check is the advisor again.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.810
And the default executor is only half price.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.306
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:43.899
I'm Ulric.

00:03:43.899 --> 00:03:44.195
Bye!
